===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To position one’s belly; to move on one’s belly.
#* {{RQ:London Call of the Wild|page=220|passage='''Bellying''' forward to the edge of the clearing, he found Hans, lying on his face, feathered with arrows like a porcupine.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[swell]] and become [[protuberant]]; to [[bulge]] or [[billow]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Homer's Ilias|book=I|page=213|passage=The Pow'r appeaſ'd, with Winds ſuffic'd the Sail, / The '''bellying''' Canvaſs ſtrutted with the Gale; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1890|author=w:Rudyard Kipling|title=The Rhyme of the Three Captains|url=http://www.bartleby.com/364/176.html
|passage=The halliards twanged against the tops, the bunting '''bellied''' broad,}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1914|author=w:Theodore Roosevelt|title=Through the Brazilian Wilderness|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/11746/pg11746-images.html|chapter=6
|passage=There were trees whose trunks '''bellied''' into huge swellings.}}
#* '''1917''' rev. '''1925''' {{w|Ezra Pound}}, "Canto I"
#*: winds from sternward
#*: Bore us onward with '''bellying''' canvas ...
#* '''1930''', {{w|Otis Adelbert Kline}}, ''The Prince of Peril'', serialized in ''[[w:Argosy (magazine)|Argosy]]'', Chapter 1,<sup>[http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks06/0601431h.html]</sup>
#*: The building stood on a circular foundation, and its walls, instead of mounting skyward in a straight line, '''bellied''' outward and then curved in again at the top.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to swell out; to [[fill]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=II|scene=ii|passage=Your breath of full consent '''bellied''' his sails; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Lewis Main Street|chapter=I|passage=A breeze which had crossed a thousand miles of wheat-lands '''bellied''' her taffeta skirt in a line so graceful, so full of animation and moving beauty, that the heart of a chance watcher on the lower road tightened to wistfulness over her quality of suspended freedom.}}

